<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonam Gyatso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1543–1588</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as 3rd Dalai Lama, Third Dalai Lama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonam Gyatso was a leading lama of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and abbot of Drepung monastery. In 1578 he met the Tümed ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a>, who gave him the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>; he is counted as the third, the title being given posthumously to two predecessors. His mission began the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 1588; his successor was found among Altan Khan's descendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso.jpg" alt="Sonam Gyatso" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown tibetan artist. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonam_Gyatso.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
